relations with Saudi Arabia  well it's a great question I mean I  think Saudi Arabia is in trouble today  because partly because of the situation  in Iraq which has inflamed the  fundamentalist views in this among the  Sunni community and so I think right now  we're at a difficult point point where  if we push too hard on the Saudis right  now I think the thinking is  you know maybe that whole house of cards  will collapse it's kind of the way we're  now treating Pakistan even though  everyone knows Osama bin Laden is in  Pakistan we don't want to push the  Government of Pakistan too hard to go  find him because we're afraid that that  government might collapse and the  something worse would come up in its  place  I think we're treating Saudi Arabia in  the same way we're afraid to push too  hard because if the royal family falls  and the fundamentalists who are rising  in power there come to power then you  know the oil spigots might get turned  off Steve Kline before we say goodbye to  your group because I know you in class  in a couple of minutes do you have a  final question or comment or one of your  students I think we're fairly satisfied  here and I know there are a lot of  questions in Denver and paste some Steve  so why don't you take those let's go  back to Pace University next question I  found it really disturbing after reading  your book that there were people in the  CIA who knew that some of the  intelligence cited in the run-up to the  war was faulty at best and just seems to  me that there was a real culture of fear  in the CIA and that it got really  politicized and that that was why  sometimes people didn't speak out and  they should have I was wondering if you  thought that was one of the main  problems and if there's anything we  could do to kind of take politics and  some of that bureaucratic nonsense out  of national security decision let me  take what she said culture of fear but  also could you add culture of groupthink  yeah well I think it was both I think  there was a culture of fear within the  CIA because they knew it became very  clear what the Bush administration  wanted what answers they wanted because  they were making public speeches about  the about WMD and about Iraq and so and  you didn't have to you would have to be  blind not to know what the White House  want what answers they wanted that  created a fear I think of people willing  to step forward and say no there is no  WMD it would be like the little boy  saying the emperor has no clothes  and so I think that it was a it would  have taken a major leap of courage act  of courage to actually come out and  raise questions about the intelligence  will stay with New York and Pace  University next question Alesia era I  thank you for being with us I actually  have a quick question do you think that  Rumsfeld and Cheney and even President  Bush had underestimated the power that  Powell was able to obtain because when  you mentioned those names Powell tends  to have more of like a is a positive  aura to him and do you think that his  resignation symbolized something about  the one-sidedness of the current  administration yeah I think what  happened was but by 2004 when he quit he  had basically given up he had been  defeated by Cheney and Rumsfeld and he  knew it and he knew that the war was  going badly and that the president had  really never listened  you know never listened to him and so I  think he kind of realized that it was  time to leave I think he had recognized  that Powell and that Cheney and Rumsfeld  had had the been dominant long before  that you described Colin Powell as a  lion in winter mm-hmm yeah he was a  great man who had the who had enormous  credibility with the American people and  if but but I think he was at the same  time at heart a military man who was  loyal to a fault and he didn't he never  tried to use the political capital that  he had and by never using it inside the  government he lost it  a couple questions in the back in Denver  Doug young did you have a question is  Doug there yeah I did just what we were  talking about sort of George Bush's role  and this whole thing he's come under a  lot of criticism as of late for being  quote out of touch the sources that you  talked to would they describe him that  way and what does that exactly mean I  don't think the people would describe  him as out of touch I think they would  say as I said earlier I think what they  would that  but they don't don't understand is that  when they have a briefing when they and  this Afghan briefing that I described is  a perfect example that he seems to  understand all the issues he seems  animated and interested and ask good  questions but that there doesn't seem to  be a lot of follow-up and that it's in  the follow-up and the implementation  where there is a breakdown and I think a  lot of people have you know never quite  understood why that happens I mean I  think Katrina is a good example of that  where he went to New Orleans promised  billions of dollars of aid and then the  the process broke down after he moved on  to something else I think that's the the  real question is as I said he sets broad  broad vision as a broad vision he sets a  broad agenda he leaves the  implementation to others and I think he  doesn't do enough follow-up an  implementation of his own you okay for a  couple more minutes yeah let's go back  to Denver Amex phone anyone that I've  missed Oh My yes we have lots of  questions okay let's take two we have  time for two okay do you want to cover  Max's question that she emailed us let's  go to the two questions let's take ten  from students her there okay how about  Andrew Hogue who's been waiting and  waiting and waiting  go ahead Andrew thank you and thanks for  joining us again just to step back a  little bit to the CIA prisons started  digress but um it's it seems like  someone mentioned earlier the Geneva  Convention and how we're kind of  stepping around that and and it seems  like any stance on human rights issues  that the United States puts out its kind  of seen as a joke how can we take any of  these seriously if if we have hidden  torture prisons around the world and and  ghost transferring prisoners etc and how  can we take a stance on on third-world  human rights issues when we're  committing them ourselves thanks Andrew  I think that's one of the problems we  now have in our international relations  is that the image of the United States  has been so badly damaged by the Abu  Ghraib scandal and by  related issues that torture and I think  it has hurt our ability to influence  international conventions and on those  kind of issues that the countervailing  thing about that though is that the  United States is a huge superpower and  so we are able to use our info and  economic influence in these  international organizations even when we  are criticized or are having image  problems ourselves it's one of the  things that I think is beginning to make  us look more like an empire around the  world is that you know we are able to  kind of uniquely create our own set of  conditions that we follow in the  interest of time one of the students who  was not able to be here today it was  going to ask you about how you feel  about the debate in this country between  national security and civil liberties  with regard to the NSA well it's it's  one of it's a classic debate in America  that goes back to the founding fathers  and the Constitution that we have a we  always have a debate over how much how  much freedom should we give up in  exchange for a safer or more secure  society and it's a debate that goes back  and forth throughout our history  depending on the circumstances during  Wars we tend to give up more civil  liberties in order to fight a war what I  think was what I've thought about a lot  recently is the fact that now there has  been more time has passed between 9/11  and today than between Pearl Harbor and  the end of World War two and so the  question I ask is at what time do  emergency measures put in place during  wartime become part of the permanent  landscape and the permanent way that we  as a society function and at some point  I think we have to have a debate an open  debate about how long do we put those  kind of measures in place one more  question from Denver and then a final  question from Pace University meg you  select the student in  how about Derek in the middle thank you  I was just wondering if you could expand  a little bit on the end of your book  where you briefly mentioned Porter Goss  and the CIA and I was wondering if you  could maybe characterize some of the  changes at the CIA right now and how his  leadership there has maybe changed its  relationship with other agencies within  the Bush administration and sort of the  direction things are headed thanks Derek  well I think I think the CI today is a  much less powerful organization  politically within the administration  than it was it's been downgraded it you  know John Negroponte a the new Director  of National Intelligence is the guy who  actually does the the briefings for the  President and who is the kind of the  figurehead man in charge of the  intelligence community the CI today is  still trying to sort out what its role  is in this world in which it is no  longer the lead agency on intelligence  and I think that has led to a lot of  people leaving the CIA in the last year  a lot of senior people have quit out of  frustration for me partly because they  don't like goss's management style or  the people around them or the fact that  the agency doesn't seem to have a clear  direction will conclude with one  question from Pace University and Sarah  Leslie has the last question of Isis and  Cheney said that this information eight  of the terrorists now that was published  and he was obviously very mad so have  you experienced any personal backlash  from the publication of this book from  government officials who are angered  that you publish this information or  have you run into any I guess friction  with government officials now that your  name  is on this book yes yeah we've gotten a  lot of hate mail from people who support  the President on this issue and say that  it was we shouldn't have printed it on  the other hand we've gotten a lot of  support from people around the country  who said that it was a public service to  print it I have not been contacted by  anybody in the government about it there  is a leak investigation underway but no  one from the government has contacted me  about that so we have covered a lot in  this last hour and 20 minutes but what  did you take away from all the work that  you put into this book how do you view  this administration and your country  good question I think the thing that  struck me the most was that there were  people a lot of people in the US  intelligence community and elsewhere in  the government who came to believe that  there was they were act secret  activities underway in the US government  that they had not seen in 30 or 40 years  and that there was a radicalization of  national security policy that I felt I  had to write about so I guess that's the  best way to put it  James Rison who writes for the New York  Times and the book that the students in  Denver and George Mason and Pace  University of read on behalf of those  students we want to thank you for  joining us it has been an interesting  hour in 20 minutes we appreciate it  thanks for having me  [Applause]
 

